🛡️ Key Security Business Units
1. 	Security Operations Center (SOC)
• 	A centralized facility where security professionals monitor and defend critical information assets.
• 	Provides real-time threat detection, incident response, and oversight of cybersecurity operations.
• 	Supports other departments like finance, HR, and marketing by protecting their data and systems.
• 	Typically found in large organizations due to the cost and complexity of setup and maintenance.
• 	Staffed with analysts, engineers, and incident responders working around the clock.
2. 	DevSecOps
• 	An evolution of DevOps that integrates security into every phase of software development.
• 	Encourages collaboration between developers, IT operations, and security teams.
• 	Promotes the “shift left” approach—embedding security early in the development lifecycle (during planning and design).
• 	Uses automation and coding to build, test, and deploy secure software faster.
• 	Treats security operations as software-driven, requiring developers to contribute to detection and monitoring tools.
3. 	Incident Response Team (CIRT / CSIRT / CERT)
• 	A dedicated team responsible for managing and responding to security incidents.
• 	Acts as the single point of contact for reporting breaches, malware outbreaks, or data leaks.
• 	May operate within the SOC or as an independent unit, depending on organizational structure.
• 	Handles containment, investigation, recovery, and post-incident analysis.
• 	Coordinates with legal, compliance, and communication teams during major incidents.
